  <wpt lat="46.483244" lon="6.909920">
    <name>Les Pléiades (above Vevey) [train fare only]</name>
    <desc>Les Pléiades station (top of the MVR rack railway)

    Trails
No bike park, no shuttle circus - just a 750 m vertical drop of forest tracks and singletrack back down to Blonay, Les Chevalleyres or Lally. Mellow, green, family-friendly gradients with a couple of steeper trail options in the woods above Blonay. Good first outing of the season, and you can extend north towards Les Paccots.

    Getting up / price
No dedicated bike pass: it is a normal public-transport ticket (Vevey &gt; Les Pleiades is a short-distance fare, roughly 12-14 CHF one way full price) plus a bike ticket, ~14 CHF for the day pass. Cheap day out.

    Open season
Open from 1 April; closed from 1 December (typical window; check the operator and conditions).

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Lausanne | Train | ~1h00

    Source: MOB transport.
    Prices, schedules, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.026524" lon="6.957526">
    <name>Chaumont (Neuchâtel) [city fare, ~5 CHF]</name>
    <desc>Chaumont - top of the La Coudre funicular

    Trails
Chaumont bike park: a proper DH line with 110+ doubles and around 20 drops, clearly aimed at experienced riders - but a chicken line runs alongside the whole way so you can pick your level. Back down to La Coudre it alternates forest doubletrack and singletrack. Open all year, which makes it the winter fallback when the Alps are shut.

    Getting up / price
The funicular is part of the Neuchatel city network (transN / Onde Verte): a few francs per ride, so this is by far the cheapest lift-served riding in the region. Runs year-round, every 15-30 min.

    Open season
Open from 1 January with no seasonal closure (normally open year-round).
Daily opening hours: 09:00-17:00
Typical daily estimate; check the operator schedule.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Lausanne | Train + funicular | ~1h10

    Source: TransN.
    Prices, schedules, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="46.359507" lon="7.002932">
    <name>Leysin - Berneuse [28 CHF / Magic Pass]</name>
    <desc>Berneuse 2048 m - top of the Leysin gondola

    Trails
Leysin Bike Park, still young and still growing. Two machine-built runs off the Berneuse: Nerine (1 km, red/black) and Les 3 Tours (0.4 km, red/black), plus a short liaison between them - steep, rocky and aimed at confident riders. A blue intermediate line is due in August 2026 and a green one in 2027. Off-park, the Endless Ride enduro descent towards Prafandaz and Roc de Veyges is the reason to come, and stage 1 of the Alpes du Leman Bike route runs from here to Les Diablerets.

    Getting up / price
Gondola + downhill day pass around 28 CHF. Free with the Magic Pass. Bike park opens 25 April 2026, daily 20 June - 23 August, weekends either side; the Tete d'Ai chairlift runs 11:30-16:00 in high summer.

    Open season
Open from 20 June; closed from 24 August (published 2026 dates).
Daily opening hours: 11:30-16:00
Published high-summer Tete d'Ai chairlift hours; other lifts vary.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Lausanne | Train | ~1h10

    Source: Leysin tourism.
    Prices, schedules, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.

    Rain sensitivity
Rain-sensitive: steep, rooty and rocky north-facing terrain turns greasy quickly and stays wet a while after rain.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="46.345636" lon="7.006709">
    <name>Leysin: Leysin-Grand-Hotel station</name>
    <desc>Where the Aigle cog railway drops you. The gondola bottom station is about 5 minutes away by bike.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="46.346555" lon="7.015547">
    <name>Leysin: Endless Ride (enduro descent)</name>
    <desc>The long natural enduro line off the Berneuse towards Prafandaz and Roc de Veyges - the reason most people come to Leysin rather than for the bike park itself.</desc>
  </wpt>
