  <wpt lat="33.819550" lon="73.132210">
    <name>Margalla Second Ridge MTB (Pakistan) [organized transport]</name>
    <desc>Hard backcountry ridge ride above Islamabad

    Trails
A long, remote mountain route from the Makhniyal side of the Margalla Hills, combining rough ridge riding, technical natural sections and substantial descents toward Masta Village, Gokina Valley or Khanpur. MTB Islamabad describes its organized Second Ridge ride as hard and suitable for experienced riders; the longer one-way hiking line loses roughly 1800 m while crossing shared, unsignposted terrain.

    Getting up / price
No lift. MTB Islamabad's June 2026 ride charged PKR 2000 for rider transport from Islamabad; this was an organized event price, not a permanent shuttle tariff. Join a current club outing or arrange a knowledgeable local guide and vehicle rather than attempting the remote route alone.

&amp;#9888;&amp;#65039; This is shared backcountry and protected forest terrain, not a maintained bike park. Give way to hikers, confirm that cycling is permitted on the selected line, carry water and repair equipment, and avoid fire closures or monsoon storms.

    Open season
Open from 1 October; closed from 1 June (conservative cooler and drier-season indication).
Daily opening hours: daylight only
There is no formal MTB season. Heat, monsoon rain, wildfire and local restrictions can make the route unsafe or inaccessible.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Islamabad | Organized vehicle / car | ~1h30

    Sources: MTB Islamabad's 2026 Second Ridge ride, long-route profile and the Islamabad Wildlife Management Board trail information. Confirm current cycling permission and route access locally.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="22.683945" lon="91.789139">
    <name>Fatikchhari Enduro Area (Bangladesh) [historic race, access unverified]</name>
    <desc>Hill-country venue of Bangladesh's documented enduro championship

    Trails
Fatikchhari Upazila hosted the 2019 Enduro Championship Bangladesh, documenting genuine timed enduro riding in the forested hills north of Chattogram. Public sources do not preserve a dependable permanent course map, trailhead or current maintenance status, so this pin marks the wider event area rather than a signed trail network.

    Getting up / price
No lift, permanent shuttle or public trail fee is documented. Contact the Bangladesh Cycling Federation or an active local MTB group for a current legal route, land permission, transport and a precise meeting point before travelling.

&amp;#9888;&amp;#65039; Do not treat the approximate pin as a trailhead. Land access, border-area rules and forest permissions can change, and old race stages may cross private or community land.

    Open season
Open from 1 November; closed from 1 May (conservative drier-season indication).
Daily opening hours: daylight only
There is no verified current operating season. Monsoon rain, flooding, heat and landslides can make hill tracks unsafe.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Chattogram | Local guide / car | ~1h30

    Sources: 2019 Enduro Championship result and location, contemporary event coverage and the Bangladesh Cycling Federation. Verify all access locally.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="44.169147" lon="8.343592">
    <name>Finale Outdoor Region (Italy) [commercial shuttle]</name>
    <desc>One of the world's defining enduro destinations

    Trails
A vast coastal network linking Finale Ligure, Pietra Ligure and the limestone ridges above the Ligurian Sea. Famous lines such as Rollercoaster, Little Champery and the NATO Base descents combine exposed rock, old military roads, Mediterranean forest and long technical stages. Finale has hosted the Enduro World Series and UCI Enduro World Cup for years and hosts the 2026 UCI Enduro and E-Enduro World Championships.

    Getting up / price
No mechanical lift or unified trail ticket. Licensed commercial shuttles and guides serve different zones, while strong riders can climb paved and gravel roads. Book a local operator because trail openings, legal access and sensible combinations change frequently.

    Open season
Open from 1 January with no formal seasonal closure.
Daily opening hours: daylight only
Avoid exceptional summer heat, saturated trails, wildfire closures and event-restricted stages. Some trails are maintained through the Finale Outdoor Region system; support the network locally.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Genoa Airport | Car / train | ~1h15
Nice Airport | Car | ~2h

    Sources: Finale Outdoor Region services and trail maintenance and the UCI 2026 World Championships announcement. Confirm current trails and shuttle zones with an authorized local operator.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="45.698370" lon="6.939470">
    <name>La Thuile Bike Park (Italy) [chairlifts]</name>
    <desc>Planibel base 2175 m - top of the Les Suches gondola

    Trails
La Thuile combines a lift-served park with a huge natural trail area totaling roughly 170 km. Its reputation comes from long, steep and physical enduro stages plus raw downhill tracks with roots, rock, changing lines and serious alpine weather. It has hosted the Enduro World Series and the UCI Downhill and Enduro World Cups.

    Getting up / price
Summer chairlifts carry bikes from the Planibel base, with regional summer tickets also connecting other Valle d'Aosta lift areas. Check the current resort tariff because products and included lifts vary by season.

    Open season
Open from 21 June; closed from 15 September (typical summer indication).
Daily opening hours: 09:30-17:00
Estimated summer lift window; verify published 2026 dates, weather closures and the live trail report.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Lausanne | Car | ~2h15
Aosta | Car / bus | ~45 min

    Sources: official La Thuile bike-park page and Valle d'Aosta tourism. Check current lift dates, tickets and trail status.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="45.711430" lon="6.949160">
    <name>La Thuile: Les Suches gondola valley station (1466 m)</name>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="45.690260" lon="6.916240">
    <name>La Thuile: Chaz Dura Express 2 top (2558 m)</name>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="49.750135" lon="-123.053588">
    <name>Squamish Trail Network (British Columbia) [no lift]</name>
    <desc>Granite slabs, rainforest roots and modern Canadian flow

    Trails
More than 300 km of riding spread across Diamond Head, Alice Lake, Valleycliffe and other zones between Vancouver and Whistler. Squamish ranges from approachable flow such as Half Nelson to steep rock slabs, wet roots and highly technical black and double-black descents. It is a foundational destination for modern enduro and freeride trail culture rather than a single bike park.

    Getting up / price
No lift and no unified trail fee. Most riding is pedal-accessed on forest roads and climbing trails; commercial guiding or vehicle support can be arranged for selected rides. Donate to SORCA and use official trailheads rather than residential shortcuts.

    Open season
Open from 1 April; closed from 1 December (typical lower-elevation indication).
Daily opening hours: daylight only
Some low trails ride through winter, while snow persists higher. Check closures, wildlife notices, logging operations and rain-related trail guidance.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Vancouver | Car | ~1h10
Whistler | Car | ~50 min

    Sources: Tourism Squamish and Squamish Off-Road Cycling Association. Check current trail reports and support local maintenance.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="43.402122" lon="-71.551450">
    <name>Highland Mountain Bike Park (New Hampshire) [82 USD]</name>
    <desc>America's dedicated lift-served gravity mountain

    Trails
Opened as the world's first lift-accessed mountain dedicated entirely to biking, Highland is renowned for progressive downhill, huge jump lines, dirt jumps, skills zones and a world-class slopestyle course. Green learning terrain and coaching sit alongside race tracks and expert freeride lines such as NE Style and the Claymore competition terrain.

    Getting up / price
The chairlift and Central Park surface lift serve the park. Published 2026 adult walk-up day passes are 68 USD midweek and 82 USD on weekends or holidays; season passes are also offered.

    Open season
Open from 23 April; closed from 16 November (published 2026 dates).
Daily opening hours: 09:00-17:00
Core hours shown; Fridays run later in spring and summer, operating days reduce outside peak season, and late fall closes at 16:00. Check the detailed calendar.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Boston | Car | ~1h30
Manchester, NH | Car | ~35 min

    Source: official 2026 passes, rates and hours. Check the live conditions report and book ahead on busy dates.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="-41.149041" lon="147.800948">
    <name>Blue Derby (Tasmania) [no lift, shuttle available]</name>
    <desc>World-premier purpose-built Tasmanian singletrack

    Trails
Thirty-five purpose-built trails around the former mining town of Derby plus the Blue Tier wilderness routes. Famous Enduro World Series stages such as Detonate, Trouty, Kumma-Gutza and Shearpin mix enormous granite boulders, dense fern forest, sculpted flow and technical rock. The network spans genuine beginner loops through elite enduro terrain.

    Getting up / price
No mechanical lift and the core trails are free to pedal. Multiple private operators sell uplift laps and longer shuttles for Blue Tier and Bay of Fires routes; prices and schedules vary, so reserve directly with an operator.

    Open season
Open from 1 January with no regular seasonal closure.
Daily opening hours: daylight only
Rain can improve some granite-based trails but severe weather, maintenance, fire danger and events can close sections. Check the official trail status before riding.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Launceston | Car | ~1h30
Hobart | Car | ~3h15

    Sources: official Blue Derby destination site and its trail and uplift overview. Check live trail status and book shuttles separately.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="42.542247" lon="1.502500">
    <name>Pal Arinsal Bike Park (Andorra) [five lifts]</name>
    <desc>Pyrenean World Cup gravity above La Massana

    Trails
More than 60 km across roughly 30 circuits with over 1000 m of vertical, including World Cup downhill terrain, technical forest lines, flow, jumps and beginner progression at Bike World. Pal Arinsal is a recurring UCI Mountain Bike World Cup venue and one of southern Europe's benchmark lift-served parks.

    Getting up / price
Five adapted lifts, including the La Massana gondola, move riders around the park. Half-day through six-day passes are sold online; check the live 2026 tariff because the official page routes prices through its booking system.

    Open season
Open from 20 June; closed from 13 October (published 2026 dates, with autumn weekends only after 13 September).
Daily opening hours: 09:30-18:00
Published peak-season hours; autumn weekends close at 17:00. The park is partially restricted during the 7-12 July 2026 World Cup period.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Andorra la Vella | Bus / car | ~15 min
Barcelona Airport | Car / bus | ~3h

    Sources: official Pal Arinsal Bike Park page and published 2026 summer hours. Check current lift and race-period access before travelling.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="28.244522" lon="83.947654">
    <name>Sarangkot / Pokhara Enduro Trails (Nepal) [guided, no lift]</name>
    <desc>Himalayan-view gravity above Pokhara

    Trails
Pokhara's best-known gravity riding links Sarangkot, Kahun Danda, Arba and the World Peace Pagoda hills. Expect long natural descents through subtropical forest, rock, dust, village paths and steep technical singletrack, with views across the Annapurna, Dhaulagiri and Manaslu ranges. Arba has been used for the Pokhara Enduro race.

    Getting up / price
No lift. Local operators offer guided rides, bike rental and vehicle support; a one-day guided Sarangkot product is available, while stronger riders can pedal roughly 800 m to the ridge. Book a local MTB specialist because trail entrances and permissions are not consistently signed.

    Open season
Open from 1 October; closed from 1 June (conservative dry-season indication).
Daily opening hours: daylight only
Winter mornings can be cold and misty. Avoid monsoon rain, landslides and saturated village trails from June through September.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Pokhara Lakeside | Guide vehicle / bike | ~35 min
Pokhara Airport | Car | ~40 min

    Sources: Nepal MTB's Pokhara enduro overview and its Sarangkot ride page. Use a current local guide and respect shared village paths.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="27.718508" lon="85.519730">
    <name>Nagarkot Enduro Trails (Nepal) [shuttle supported, no lift]</name>
    <desc>Kathmandu Valley's established enduro zone

    Trails
Natural singletrack drops from the Nagarkot ridge through pine forest, terraces and villages toward the Kathmandu Valley. Guided Himalayan enduro itineraries use the area for repeated technical descents, mixing flow, roots, rock steps and tight local paths. It is a dispersed shared network rather than a signed park.

    Getting up / price
No lift. Specialist operators run guided, shuttle-supported days from Kathmandu; self-guided route finding is difficult and can lead onto hiking paths or private land. Confirm the exact descent and vehicle arrangement when booking.

    Open season
Open from 1 October; closed from 1 June (conservative dry-season indication).
Daily opening hours: daylight only
Monsoon rain causes mud, erosion and landslide risk. Give way to walkers, villagers and livestock on every shared trail.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Kathmandu | Guide vehicle | ~1h30

    Sources: shuttle-supported Himalaya Enduro itinerary and Himalayan Single Track, a Kathmandu-based MTB operator. Confirm current routes and permissions with a local guide.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="28.783810" lon="83.730495">
    <name>Lower Mustang Enduro (Nepal) [expedition, no lift]</name>
    <desc>High-altitude desert singletrack around Jomsom

    Trails
Lower Mustang is Nepal's premier big-mountain enduro landscape: fast dry singletrack, loose rock, exposed jeep tracks and technical ancient paths beneath the Annapurna and Dhaulagiri massifs. Signature objectives include Lupra, Ghyaula Pass and long descents around Jomsom and Muktinath; advanced expeditions may continue toward Thorong La at extreme altitude.

    Getting up / price
No lift. Access requires pedalling, hike-a-bike and support vehicles where roads permit. Book a specialist expedition covering permits, acclimatization, guiding, lodging and mechanical support; this is not a trail-center day ride.

&amp;#9888;&amp;#65039; Altitude, exposure, wind, cold, remote rescue and rapidly changing trail or permit rules are serious hazards. Acclimatize properly and do not attempt high passes unsupported.

    Open season
Open from 1 April; closed from 1 December (broad expedition window).
Daily opening hours: daylight only
Spring and autumn are preferred. Winter snow and cold close high routes; summer monsoon impacts are lower than elsewhere in Nepal but flights, roads and river crossings remain weather-sensitive.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Pokhara | Flight / expedition vehicle | ~25 min flight to Jomsom

    Sources: Nepal MTB's Mustang enduro guide and guided Himalayan enduro expedition. Verify permits, altitude plan and transport with a licensed operator.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="18.816608" lon="98.892360">
    <name>Doi Suthep-Pui Downhill (Chiang Mai, Thailand) [shuttle, no lift]</name>
    <desc>Northern Thailand's established jungle downhill hub

    Trails
A collection of technical natural descents on Doi Suthep and Doi Pui above Chiang Mai, ranging from guided introductory downhill to steep expert lines with roots, rock, ruts and slippery tropical soil. Trails cross national-park and sacred landscapes, are not consistently signed and have had access disputes, so current local guidance matters.

    Getting up / price
No mechanical lift. Trailhead publishes a private shuttle at 4800 THB per day for up to six experienced riders, excluding bikes, guide, park fees and insurance; guided downhill packages with bikes and protection are also available. The final shuttle lap must start by 15:30.

    Open season
Open from 1 November; closed from 1 April (preferred cool and dry-season indication).
Daily opening hours: 08:00-16:00
Estimated riding window based on shuttle operation. Smoke season, extreme heat, monsoon mud and national-park closures can make trails unsuitable.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Chiang Mai Old City | Booked shuttle | ~45 min

    Sources: Trailhead shuttle service and price, guided downhill tour and local trail-access context. Confirm every trail with a reputable bicycle guide.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="19.888744" lon="102.135898">
    <name>Luang Prabang Jungle MTB (Laos) [guided, no lift]</name>
    <desc>Remote jungle singletrack and long natural descents

    Trails
The mountains outside Luang Prabang contain steep village tracks, dense-jungle singletrack and rough river-valley links. The one-day Lung Buster route climbs roughly 1100 m before a substantial descent over about 35 km, while multi-day northern itineraries continue through isolated mountain and tribal communities. This is demanding adventure trail riding, not a maintained bike park.

    Getting up / price
No lift or permanent shuttle. The published guided Lung Buster day starts around 72 USD and includes a bike and helmet; longer supported tours with luggage transport are available. Use a guide because paths are remote, unsigned and pass through communities unfamiliar with independent riders.

    Open season
Open from 1 October; closed from 1 May (drier-season indication).
Daily opening hours: daylight only
Rainy-season mud, swollen river crossings, heat and limited rescue access make independent riding unsuitable.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Luang Prabang centre | Guide transfer / bike | Tour dependent

    Sources: Tiger Trail's Lung Buster MTB route and guided Northern Laos singletrack expedition. Book with an established operator and respect village access.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="13.620273" lon="104.116609">
    <name>Phnom Kulen MTB (Cambodia) [guided, no lift]</name>
    <desc>Sacred sandstone mountain riding north of Siem Reap

    Trails
Phnom Kulen mixes red dirt, sandy jungle tracks, stone, village paths and a roughly 12 km guided downhill from the plateau toward the lowlands. The mountain hosted the mountain-bike events of the 2023 Southeast Asian Games, but ordinary visitor routes are shared and do not form a permanently signed gravity park.

    Getting up / price
No lift. Guided tours use vehicle support from Siem Reap and combine plateau riding with the long descent. National-park admission and tour pricing vary; book a bicycle-specific guide who can confirm the legal route and transport.

&amp;#9888;&amp;#65039; Phnom Kulen is a national park and sacred cultural landscape. Stay on the guide-approved route, yield to walkers and residents, and never ride temple steps or archaeological features.

    Open season
Open from 1 November; closed from 1 May (drier-season indication).
Daily opening hours: daylight only
Monsoon rain makes sandstone, roots and river crossings hazardous. Heat is severe even in the dry season, so start early and carry ample water.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Siem Reap | Guide vehicle | ~1h30

    Sources: guided Kulen mountain-bike and downhill route and the 2023 SEA Games venue record. Confirm park fees, route permission and conditions locally.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="65.660600" lon="-18.107500">
    <name>Hlíðarfjall Bike Park (Iceland) [chairlifts]</name>
    <desc>Iceland's only lift-served bike park

    Trails
Purpose-built downhill and enduro trails descend the open volcanic slopes above Akureyri, with connections toward Glerárdalur and Kjarnaskógur. The lower Fjarkinn chair serves the main park and selected summer weekends may also use Fjallkona, extending lift access above 1000 m. Expect exposed terrain, fast weather changes and a compact mix of flow and technical natural ground.

    Getting up / price
Fjarkinn is the regular bike-carrying summer chairlift; upper-lift operation is more limited. Tickets, operating days and which trails are open vary by season, so check the resort before travelling.

    Open season
Open from 10 July; closed from 8 September (typical published summer window).
Daily opening hours: 10:00-17:00
Estimated daytime hours. Summer lifts commonly run only Thursday-Sunday and the upper chair on selected weekends. Wind, rain or residual snow can stop operations.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Akureyri | Car | ~10 min

    Sources: official Hlíðarfjall site and Visit Iceland's bike-park and summer-lift overview. Confirm the current lift calendar and weather status.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="66.962496" lon="-53.716119">
    <name>Sisimiut Backcountry Trails (Greenland) [no lift]</name>
    <desc>Arctic dirt tracks and technical wilderness riding

    Trails
East of Sisimiut, the road dissolves into a network of rocky dirt tracks suitable for mountain bikes, with options toward the valleys beneath Palasip Qaqqaa and the western end of the Arctic Circle Trail. Riding is raw, shared and unsigned rather than a managed bike park; terrain ranges from accessible dry tracks near town to hike-a-bike and serious remote travel.

    Getting up / price
No lift or permanent shuttle. Bikes may be borrowed or rented locally and guided town or wilderness outings are available. Use local advice before leaving the near-town tracks because there is no road connection, phone coverage or rapid rescue in the backcountry.

&amp;#9888;&amp;#65039; Do not assume marked hiking trails are rideable throughout. Protect fragile tundra, yield to walkers, avoid wet ground and turn back before terrain or river crossings exceed your expedition skills.

    Open season
Open from 1 July; closed from 15 September (conservative snow-free indication).
Daily opening hours: daylight only
There is no formal operating season. Snow, saturated tundra, mosquitoes and river levels vary sharply; obtain current local conditions.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Sisimiut centre | Bike | ~15 min to dirt tracks

    Sources: Visit Greenland's official biking guide and its Sisimiut destination and trail overview. Ask a local operator which tracks are currently appropriate to ride.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="67.008677" lon="-50.691336">
    <name>Kangerlussuaq Arctic MTB (Greenland) [no lift]</name>
    <desc>Hardcore tundra riding from town toward the ice sheet

    Trails
Greenland's longest connected road and surrounding rough tracks make Kangerlussuaq its most convenient mountain-bike base. The signature objective runs roughly 25 km from town toward Russell Glacier or Point 660 at the ice-sheet edge over rocky, sandy and rolling glacial terrain. This is remote adventure riding rather than gravity laps, with musk oxen and reindeer possible along the route.

    Getting up / price
No lift. Ride out-and-back, rent locally or arrange a guide and vehicle support. Distances, loose surfaces, wind and a complete lack of services make even the road ride a substantial backcountry undertaking.

&amp;#9888;&amp;#65039; Carry repair equipment, emergency layers, food and communication suitable for Arctic wilderness. Keep a large distance from wildlife and never approach glacier fronts or the ice sheet without expert guidance.

    Open season
Open from 15 June; closed from 15 September (conservative summer indication).
Daily opening hours: daylight only
There is no formal operating season. Snowmelt, dust, wind and rapidly changing cold weather determine whether the route is practical.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Kangerlussuaq Airport | Bike | Immediate

    Source: Visit Greenland's official Kangerlussuaq biking guide. Confirm rental, road condition and safe turnaround plans locally.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="61.141048" lon="-149.738648">
    <name>Hilltop Bike Park (Alaska) [chairlift, season pass]</name>
    <desc>North America's northernmost lift-served bike park

    Trails
A compact, professionally built gravity park inside Anchorage with nine trails across 294 vertical feet. Beginner flow progresses toward advanced technical and jump lines, backed by a skills area, pump track and airbag zone. Its modest vertical makes repeated training laps the attraction rather than long alpine descents.

    Getting up / price
One triple chairlift carries bikes. The published season pass starts at 300 USD and pedal-only access to trails and airbags starts at 10 USD; current day-ticket pricing should be checked online.

    Open season
Open from 1 June; closed from 1 October (typical summer indication).
Daily opening hours: 13:00-20:00
Published core bike-park hours; scheduled operating days and special-event hours vary. Complete the current waiver and check trail conditions before arrival.

    Access
