  <wpt lat="44.872750" lon="-123.495705">
    <name>Black Rock Mountain Bike Area (Oregon) [no lift]</name>
    <desc>Black Rock MTB parking, ~215 m

    Trails
Purpose-built freeride and DH lines on Mt. Brown in the Coast Range. Banzai Downhill is a rollable green/blue flow line with tabletops and ride-arounds; Sickter Gnar is a black mix of drops, jumps, rocks and tech. The signature expert-only trails are Granny's Kitchen, Sunday Stroll, Brake Check, The Runaround and Player Slayer, with wooden features, mandatory gaps and big-freeride moves. Basic Training is an all-levels skills area for drops and jumps.

    Getting up / price
No lift - all climbing is under your own power. Parking at the trailhead is free. BRMBA is a volunteer-run 501(c)(3) nonprofit; donations support trail building and maintenance.

    Open season
Open year-round. Coast Range winters are wet and trails can become muddy; actual rideability depends on weather and conditions.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Portland | Car | ~1h55
Salem | Car | ~55 min
McMinnville | Car | ~1h05

    Sources: BRMBA trails, BRMBA about us, BRMBA rider conduct, ODF recreation. Verify conditions, rules and access before riding.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="37.997046" lon="-122.612537">
    <name>Tamarancho - Fairfax, CA [no lift]</name>
    <desc>Camp Tamarancho trailhead at 230 m - permit-based natural singletrack in Marin County

    Trails
An ~11-mile loop of hand-built, bike-legal singletrack on Boy Scouts of America property west of Fairfax. The clockwise loop climbs Alchemist Trail (uphill only as of 2026), then Goldman, Serpentine, Wagon Wheel and B-17 trails. Broken Dam Trail closes the loop. Endor Trail is a short, downhill-only flow trail with high berms and jumps; Caballo Rojo is another downhill-only option. Expect roots, rocks, switchbacks and the famous rock on Serpentine; Wagon Wheel adds rock gardens. Difficulty ranges from beginner-friendly sections to intermediate/advanced, with a few expert features.

    Getting up / price
No lift. Park in Fairfax and ride ~1.5 miles up Iron Springs Road to the trailhead kiosk. A permit is required and must be visible while riding. Day pass $6; annual single $50; family annual $75; Gold single $95; Gold family $135. Buy online from the Marin Council, BSA, or get day passes in person at Sunshine Bicycle Center in Fairfax. Class-1 eMTB only; no dogs; no riding when wet or muddy; no parking on Iron Springs Road.

    Open season
Open year-round; closed when trails are wet, raining or muddy after storms.
Daily opening hours: daylight hours

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Fairfax town center | Bike | ~15 min climb
San Francisco | Car | ~1 h
Oakland | Car | ~45 min

    Sources: Marin Council BSA - Tamarancho Mtn-Biking, Marin Trail Stewards - Tamarancho Trail System, Tamarancho Report - Passes, MTB Project - Camp Tamarancho Loop and Bay Area Rides - Camp Tamarancho.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="38.001710" lon="-122.462600">
    <name>China Camp State Park (California) [no lift]</name>
    <desc>Pedal-accessed Bay Area trail system from sea level to ~320 m on San Pablo Bay

    Trails
About 15 miles of multi-use trails wind through oak woodland, salt marsh and grassland. The Turtle Back Nature Trail is an easy, hiker-only ADA loop. For bikes, the Shoreline/Bayview Loop (6.9 mi, moderate) is the classic circuit, while Bay View/Bay Hills/Back Ranch Fire Trail (5.6 mi, strenuous) climbs San Pedro Mountain to ~320 m with views of Mount Tamalpais, Mount Diablo and the Coast Range. Terrain ranges from mellow fire road to rocky, rutted descents.

    Getting up / price
No lift. Pedal up on park roads and fire roads. Vehicle day-use fee is $5 per car; annual California State Parks passes are accepted. Friends of China Camp annual pass/membership also available.

    Open season
Open year-round, 8 a.m. to sunset daily. Coastal-temperate climate: winter ~10-15 C, summer ~20-30 C; mornings can be foggy.
Class I pedal-assist e-bikes only, max 15 mph (5 mph on blind corners and when passing). Dogs are not allowed on trails.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
San Francisco | Car via Hwy 101 / N. San Pedro Rd | ~45 min
San Rafael | Car / bike | ~15 min / direct
China Camp Village trailhead | Bike | Direct

    Sources: California State Parks - China Camp State Park, Friends of China Camp - Activities and Trails, Friends of China Camp - E-bike rules, OpenStreetMap/Nominatim and OpenTopoData for coordinates and elevation.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="37.411000" lon="-122.305900">
    <name>Skeggs Point / El Corte de Madera Creek Preserve [no lift]</name>
    <desc>Skeggs Point 709 m - trailhead

    Trails
El Corte de Madera Creek Preserve, locally called Skeggs Point, is a 2,908-acre redwood-forest network with 34+ miles of multi-use trail (about 20 miles singletrack and 14 miles fire road). Bike-legal highlights include Sierra Morena, Oljon, Resolution, North Leaf, Manzanita, Giant Salamander, Blue Blossom, Fir, Methuselah and Timberview. Difficulty ranges from mellow intermediate singletrack to steep, technical, rocky sections that many riders hike-a-bike; Fir Trail and some others are really only fun downhill. Helmets are required, the trail speed limit is 15 mph, and e-bikes are not allowed. Check current trail status before riding, as some trails close seasonally for wet weather or salamander protection.

    Getting up / price
No lift - every climb is earned. Park free at the Skeggs Point vista-point lot (26 spaces, across Skyline Blvd from the trailhead) or at the El Corte de Madera Parking Area (65 spaces, about 0.4 mi south). Restrooms are available at the main El Corte de Madera lot. Bring a map or GPS; the dense network is easy to get lost in.

    Open season
Open a half-hour before sunrise until a half-hour after sunset, year-round. Expect heavy coastal fog and winter wet-weather closures, especially on Giant Salamander Trail. Verify current conditions on the Midpen website before heading out.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
San Francisco | Car | ~55 min
Redwood City | Car | ~25 min
San Jose | Car | ~35 min

    Sources: Midpeninsula Regional Open Space District, MTB Project, SF Bay Area Mountain Biking, Bay Area Mountain Bike Rides.
    Prices, schedules, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="38.454418" lon="-122.636526">
    <name>Trione-Annadel State Park (Santa Rosa, CA) [no lift]</name>
    <desc>Channel Drive entrance, ~120 m (~400 ft)

    Trails
Pedal-accessed state park with 35-45 miles of multi-use trails through volcanic rock, basalt outcrops and old cobblestone quarry terrain. Riding mixes oak woodland, grassland, chaparral and riparian zones. Lake Ilsanjo and Ledson Marsh sit inside the park; Bennett Mountain tops out at 575 m (1,887 ft). Popular routes include the Canyon Trail, South Burma and ridges above Lake Ilsanjo. Natural, rocky and loose volcanic surfaces mean the riding suits confident intermediates and experts; a few easier fire roads and the Channel Trail are more beginner-friendly.

    Getting up / price
No lift. You pedal up the fire roads and ridge trails. Vehicle day-use fee is $8 (regular auto) paid at the Visitor Center on Channel Drive or via the LAZ app; several California State Parks annual passes are accepted. No camping inside the park; nearest camping is Spring Lake Regional Park and Sugarloaf Ridge State Park. E-bikes are considered motorized vehicles and are not allowed on trails.

    Open season
Open year-round 8 AM to sunset, no overnight use. Best riding is spring through fall; summers are warm and winters are cool and muddy after rain. Peak wildflower season is April to June.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Santa Rosa | Car / bike | ~10 min
San Francisco | Car | ~1 h 15 min
Kenwood (Lawndale Rd trailhead) | Car | ~15 min to park

    Sources: California State Parks - Trione-Annadel State Park and Wikipedia - Trione-Annadel State Park. Dogs are only allowed on paved areas; check current trail closures and dam-work notices before visiting.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="46.259716" lon="7.635547">
    <name>Leuk / Illalpe - Meretsch [no lift]</name>
    <desc>Illalpe / Meretsch natural enduro area

    Trails
Natural, technical descents on the sunny slopes above Leuk, including the 5.9 km Main descent, the 1.6 km Meretschytrail and a 1.2 km grade-5 Expert descent. These are separate mountain paths rather than a lift-served bike park; plan a pedal-up ride or arrange a local shuttle, and check access, conditions and trail-sharing rules before riding.

    Getting up / price
No documented bike lift. Pedal or arrange a local shuttle. Free trail access where permitted.

    Open season
Open from 01 May; closed from 01 November (typical snow-free window; verify local conditions).

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Leuk | Car / bike | Local access
Susten | Train + bike | Local access

    Sources: Leuk Tourism MTB information, OSM Main descent, OSM Meretschytrail and OSM Expert descent. Prices, access, trail status and local rules can change; verify before riding.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="46.229171" lon="7.609693">
    <name>St-Luc Bike Park / Tignousa (Val d'Anniviers) [29 CHF]</name>
    <desc>Tignousa 2182 m - top of the St-Luc-Tignousa funicular

    Trails
Three maintained, purpose-built DH runs descend about 500 vertical metres from Tignousa to St-Luc. The park totals about 7 km and combines berms, wooden features, jumps and natural forest terrain.

    - Ginette: 2.5 km blue beginner run, wide and playful.
- Funi Racer: 2.5 km red race run used for the Swiss DH Championships.
- Black Lion: 2.2 km expert black run with a jump line below the Bunny Up Park.
- Bunny Up Park: Two table-jump lines, one red and one black, at the La Foret car park.

    The official 24.7 km Weisshorn Bike loop is also traced on the map. It can be shortened with the funicular and combines the Planet Trail, Hotel Weisshorn and a scenic descent through Copate.

    Getting up / price
The St-Luc-Tignousa funicular carries bikes every 30 minutes. The 2026 one-day bike-park adult fare is 29 CHF. Magic Pass 2026-2027 includes the Val d'Anniviers summer lifts and bike transport.

    Open season
Open from 23 May; closed from 1 November.
Daily opening hours: 08:00-17:00, extended to 18:00 from 4 July to 23 August 2026.
Check current trail status and the operator timetable before travelling.

    Access
From | Transport | Duration
Sierre | Car / bus | ~30 min
Sion | Car | ~40 min
Visp | Car | ~45 min

    Sources: Val d'Anniviers - Bike Park St-Luc and Valais/Wallis Promotion.
    Prices, schedules, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="46.219634" lon="7.602517">
    <name>St-Luc: funicular valley station</name>
    <desc>St-Luc station at 1684 m, base of the bike-carrying funicular to Tignousa.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <trk>
    <name>St-Luc: Weisshorn Bike</name>
    <desc>24.7 km intermediate MTB loop with about 815 m of climbing and descending. From St-Luc it climbs through the Rochet and Teppes forests to Tignousa, follows the panoramic Planet Trail towards the historic Hotel Weisshorn, then returns to Chiesso before descending through Copate. The ascent can be shortened by taking the bike-carrying funicular to Tignousa. Source: Valais/Wallis Promotion - Weisshorn Bike. Official GPX geometry supplied by Valais/Wallis Promotion via Outdooractive, simplified to 12 m.</desc>
    <trkseg>
