  <wpt lat="21.859600" lon="-102.247200">
    <name>Arkytox Bike Park (Aguascalientes) [no lift]</name>
    <desc>Trailhead base 1,890 m

    Trails
A community-driven mountain bike park specialising in MTB Enduro riding. The network features a well-balanced mix of technical handling sections and fast, flowy tracks suitable for progressing riders. Built and maintained by the active local trail-building community, Arkytox offers fun, accessible terrain in a collaborative atmosphere.

    Getting up / price
No lift. Pedal-up / self-shuttle access. Free entry, open 24 hours daily.

    Open season
Open year-round.
Daily opening hours: 24 hours

    Peak times: Sunday mornings and weekend afternoons. Least crowded: Monday and Thursday evenings.

    Access

      From | Transport | Duration

      Aguascalientes | Car | ~20 min

      Mexico City | Car | ~5 h 30

    Sources: local trail information and community riding reports. Trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="21.849100" lon="-102.392000">
    <name>Yuca Xtreme Bike Park (Aguascalientes) [100 MXN]</name>
    <desc>Bottom shuttle base 2,580 m

    Trails
High-altitude forested bike park in the Sierra Fría, featuring 12 designated trails primarily designed for All-Mountain and Downhill disciplines. Standout lines include Bosquecito, a highly rated flowy blue track with rock gardens, and Camino Ancho Yuca Xtreme, a challenging green line featuring a steep black-diamond style climb. The park is fully shuttle-accessible for easy uplifts through pine forest terrain.

    Getting up / price
Vehicular shuttle trucks. Day ticket approximately 100 MXN; confirm current pricing. Private property park requiring day-pass purchase.

    Open season
Open from 1 April; closed from 1 November (typical season for high-altitude forest riding).
Daily opening hours: 09:00-18:00

    Estimated hours; check with the operator for current shuttle schedules and reservation requirements.

    Access

      From | Transport | Duration

      Aguascalientes | Car | ~1 h 15

      Mexico City | Car | ~6 h

    Sources: regional trail network documentation and shuttle operator information. Prices, schedules and shuttle availability can change; verify before travel.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="18.411600" lon="-97.164300">
    <name>Murciélagos Bike Park (Puebla) [check operator]</name>
    <desc>Bottom shuttle base / Hostelería El Trébol 1,690 m

    Trails
Steep mountainside bike park in Ajalpan, Puebla, featuring 10 sustainable trails built for Enduro and Downhill mountain biking. The network is fully optimised to support e-bikes. Standout descents include Coyote, a massive 5-mile long downhill singletrack featuring switchbacks, tight speed segments and 3,416 feet of raw descent, plus Aguacate, a fast, highly technical rock-strewn trail.

    Getting up / price
Vehicular shuttle trucks and e-bike friendly. Riders check in at Hostelería El Trébol hostel, where maintenance fees are paid and vehicle shuttles, local guides or lodging can be arranged. Price not verified here; check the operator.

    Open season
Open from 1 April; closed from 1 November (estimated typical season).
Daily opening hours: 08:00-18:00

    Estimated hours; confirm current shuttle schedules and hostel services with the operator.

    Access

      From | Transport | Duration

      Puebla | Car | ~2 h

      Mexico City | Car | ~3 h 30

    Sources: local trail network information and hostel operator reports. Prices, schedules, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="20.203600" lon="-98.718500">
    <name>Bike Park KRB (Hidalgo) [290 MXN]</name>
    <desc>Bottom shuttle base and parking 2,650 m

    Trails
Dense pine-forest bike park in Mineral del Chico, Hidalgo, holding 15 distinct routes tailored specifically for intermediate to advanced Enduro and Downhill progression. The landscape is incredibly steep and chunky, packed with wooden bridges, high-speed drops and tight natural rock gardens. A serious destination for technical riding in scenic sierra terrain.

    Getting up / price
Vehicular shuttle trucks. Standard Sunday group shuttle approximately 290 MXN including pass and shuttle; confirm current pricing. On-site cabin lodging available.

    Open season
Open year-round.
Daily opening hours: 09:00-17:00

    Published operating hours; shuttle schedules may vary. Confirm group shuttle days and reservation requirements.

    Access

      From | Transport | Duration

      Mexico City | Car | ~2 h 30

      Pachuca | Car | ~1 h

    Sources: park shuttle operator information and regional trail network documentation. Prices, schedules, trail status and shuttle availability can change; verify before travel.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="20.627700" lon="-100.490100">
    <name>Meteoro Bike Park (Querétaro) [40 MXN parking]</name>
    <desc>Main parking and trailhead base 1,850 m

    Trails
Prominent regional MTB hub heavily supported by the local mountain-bike community and the Sonic's MTB team. Features over 8 designated tracks ranging from beginner flow to advanced progression lines. The network is highly modular, packed with built-in features including jump progression clinics, wallrides and progressive drop zones. Free access to the trails with a small parking fee.

    Getting up / price
No lift. Pedal-up / self-shuttle access. Entry to the trail network is free; secure vehicle parking at the base approximately 40 MXN.

    Open season
Open year-round.
Daily opening hours: 07:00-20:00

    Estimated typical hours; trail network is accessible during daylight.

    Access

      From | Transport | Duration

      Querétaro | Car | ~30 min

      Mexico City | Car | ~2 h 30

    Sources: local MTB community reports and regional trail network information. Prices, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="27.519500" lon="-107.830700">
    <name>Parque Barrancas Trail Network (San Rafael) [no lift]</name>
    <desc>Top trailhead hub 2,120 m - canyon edge

    Trails
Grand canyon enduro network in the dramatic San Rafael area of Chihuahua, frequently associated with the sweeping Copper Canyon region landscape. Built primarily as an Enduro and rugged All-Mountain network featuring raw, highly technical singletracks plunging down dramatic canyon topography. Standout lines include Ruta Panorámica, a scenic 7-mile blue singletrack lined with berms and ladder bridges, and Ruta Extrema El Lomo, a gnarly double-black diamond raw ridge descent dropping 1,901 vertical feet.

    Getting up / price
No lift. Pedal-up or rugged point-to-point vehicle drops. Free access to natural trail network.

    Open season
Open from 1 April; closed from 1 November (estimated typical season for high-altitude canyon riding).
Daily opening hours: daylight hours

    Remote terrain; carry navigation equipment and verify current trail conditions locally.

    Access

      From | Transport | Duration

      Chihuahua | Car | ~4 h

      San Rafael village | Bike / 4x4 | Direct

    Sources: regional trail network documentation and rider reports. Trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="21.082700" lon="-101.218500">
    <name>La Fragua Bike Park (Guanajuato) [50 MXN]</name>
    <desc>Bottom shuttle base 2,230 m

    Trails
Private mountain property bike park high in the Sierra de Santa Rosa outside Guanajuato City. Features 13 technical trails tailored for All-Mountain, Enduro and Downhill disciplines. Boasts a massive 8,335 feet of total accumulated descent starting from a high peak elevation of 9,357 feet. Standout lines include Carbon Flow, a fast, heavily sculpted line featuring gap jumps, berms and wooden bridges, plus Cañada, a steep rock-strewn black diamond downhill-only singletrack. Highly rated on-site restaurant serving local food.

    Getting up / price
Vehicular shuttle trucks; e-bikes allowed. Requires private day-ticket pass approximately 50 MXN; confirm current pricing.

    Open season
Open from 1 April; closed from 1 November (typical season for high-sierra riding).
Daily opening hours: 09:00-18:00

    Estimated hours; confirm current shuttle schedules and day-pass requirements with the operator.

    Access

      From | Transport | Duration

      Guanajuato City | Car | ~45 min

      Mexico City | Car | ~4 h

    Sources: park operator information and regional trail network documentation. Prices, schedules, trail status and shuttle availability can change; verify before travel.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="20.575600" lon="-87.142000">
    <name>Punta Venado Bike Park (Playa del Carmen) [510 MXN]</name>
    <desc>Main basecamp and shop - coastal highway Km 278

    Trails
Premier specialised mountain bike park in the Yucatan Peninsula. Contains 20 singletrack trails weaving through coastal mangroves and thick jungle. Unlike mountainous parks, this is a Cross-Country (XC) network built entirely over rugged, unforgiving fossilised coral reefs, simulating high technical handling without major elevation. Standout lines include Del Jaguar #7 and the Earthquake Trail, both physical, punchy lines packed with jagged limestone rock gardens. Loops uniquely finish directly on a Caribbean beach.

    Getting up / price
No lift. Strictly pedal-powered cross-country over completely flat landscape. Standard international trail access approximately 510 MXN (~$30 USD), with premium full-suspension bike rentals available on-site.

    Open season
Open year-round; closed Mondays.
Daily opening hours: 07:00-15:00 (Tuesday to Sunday)

    Published operating hours; closed on Mondays. Confirm current schedules and rental availability.

    Access

      From | Transport | Duration

      Playa del Carmen | Car | ~15 min

      Cancún | Car | ~50 min

    Sources: park operator official information and regional trail network documentation. Prices, schedules, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="-3.825000" lon="-38.648900">
    <name>Terra na Veia Bike Park (Ceará) [30 BRL]</name>
    <desc>783-hectare natural estate near Maranguape

    Trails
Northeast Brazil's premier MTB destination with approximately 30 km of purpose-built singletracks across more than 20 trails. Trail network ranges from beginner-friendly green routes to challenging black-diamond downhill and enduro lines. Features include fast berms, sequenced jump lines (notably Trilha 6b), rock gardens and wooden bridge crossings through native tropical vegetation.

    Getting up / price
No mechanical lift. Access is pedal-up or push-up through the climbing routes. Day-use pass approximately 30 BRL.

    Open season
Open year-round (Ceará's tropical climate provides consistent 12-month access).
Daily opening hours: daylight hours

    Operating hours vary; check park status before visiting.

    Access

      From | Transport | Duration

      Fortaleza | Car | ~40 min

      Maranguape | Car / bike | ~15 min

    Sources: Regional MTB network documentation and operator information. Prices, schedules, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="-25.462100" lon="-49.712100">
    <name>Águas da Serra Bike Park (Paraná) [20-30 BRL]</name>
    <desc>São Luiz do Purunã, Balsa Nova / Campo Largo region

    Trails
Located on the private Estância Águas da Serra, this venue is renowned for its technical Downhill (DH) and Enduro terrain. Four main pistas (Pista 1 through Pista 4) deliver steep off-cambers, deep ruts and gullies (valetas), high-speed gap jumps and technical rock features including the iconic Drop do Pedrão. A proven testing ground for serious gravity riders.

    Getting up / price
No chairlift or shuttle. Riders push or pedal back to the top. Day pass fee approximately 20-30 BRL.

    Open season
Open year-round, primarily active on weekends and holidays.
Daily opening hours: weekend and holiday hours

    Typically open weekends and public holidays; confirm access before visiting.

    Access

      From | Transport | Duration

      Curitiba | Car | ~50 min

      Campo Largo | Car | ~25 min

    Sources: Regional MTB community documentation and venue operator information. Prices, schedules, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="-25.238300" lon="-48.836800">
    <name>Canal Aventura Bike Park (Paraná) [20-40 BRL]</name>
    <desc>Atlantic Forest riding at the Serra do Mar foothills

    Trails
Set in the dense Atlantic Forest near Curitiba, this park features trails named after regional wildlife: Jaguarundi, Macaco Prego, Urubu-rei and Graxaim. The terrain blends flow sections with technical challenges including mud, wet roots, off-camber chutes, small gap jumps and wooden bridge crossings. Best during the drier months from April through October.

    Getting up / price
No mechanical lift. Ascent is accomplished by pedaling or pushing up the climbing routes. Occasional shuttles organized for special race events. Standard day-use entry approximately 20-40 BRL.

    Open season
Open year-round, best conditions from April through October.
Daily opening hours: weekends and holidays

    Typically open weekends and holidays; drier season (April-October) offers best trail conditions.

    Access

      From | Transport | Duration

      Curitiba | Car | ~30 min

      Piraquara | Car / bike | ~10 min

    Sources: Canal Aventura and regional MTB documentation. Prices, schedules, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="-25.399500" lon="-49.296600">
    <name>Vista Alegre Bike Park (Curitiba) [free, no lift]</name>
    <desc>Urban downhill and freeride in Vista Alegre / Pilarzinho neighborhood

    Trails
An urban gravity spot inside Curitiba city limits. Features shorter, fast-paced downhill tracks including Pista do Parque, Pista da Cachoeira and Perfume de Gardênio. Routes start near the São José church and descend toward the park's waterfall area, built with berms, rollers, small gap jumps and tight tree-lined singletrack. A local favorite for quick sessions.

    Getting up / price
No lift or shuttle. Requires pedaling or pushing back to the top. Free access (public city park trails).

    Open season
Open year-round (public access area).
Daily opening hours: daylight hours

    Public park trails; respect local regulations and trail closures.

    Access

      From | Transport | Duration

      Curitiba downtown | Bike | ~20 min

      Curitiba | Public transport + bike | ~30 min

    Sources: Local MTB community documentation and Curitiba municipal trail information. Prices, schedules, trail status and access can change; verify before travel.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="46.101349" lon="7.073844">
    <name>Martigny / Vallee du Trient MTB [no lift]</name>
    <desc>Martigny route trailhead - pedal-powered loops

    Trails
Two substantial official MTB loops start in Martigny. Le Planard is a 27 km intermediate circuit with about 1,065 m of climbing over Mont Chemin and the Col des Planches. The 40.5 km Tour de la Vallee du Trient is a difficult all-day route with about 1,705 m of climbing through Vernayaz, Les Marecottes, Le Tretien, Finhaut and the Col de la Forclaz. These are mixed-surface mountain tours rather than purpose-built downhill runs; expect shared paths and roads.

    Getting up
There is no verified bike-park uplift for these routes. Both are designed as pedal-powered loops from Martigny.

    Open season
Le Planard is generally suitable from spring through autumn. The higher Trient loop is published for early July to mid-October; check snow and trail conditions.

    Source: Le Planard and Tour de la Vallee du Trient, Martigny Tourism.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <trk>
    <name>Martigny: Le Planard</name>
    <desc>27 km intermediate MTB loop with about 1,065 m of climbing. It climbs Mont Chemin through Chemin-Dessous to the Col des Planches, crosses the Planard pasture, descends towards Charrat and returns to Martigny along the shared Bisse du Guercet. Source: Martigny Tourism. Official GPX geometry supplied by Martigny Tourism, simplified to 15 m.</desc>
