  <wpt lat="-15.772000" lon="-68.646000">
    <name>Sorata MTB (Bolivia) [no lift]</name>
    <desc>High-altitude mountain biking around Sorata, with steep valley roads, footpaths and downhill routes below the Cordillera Real. This is a local/backcountry riding area, not a managed bike park. Verify access and hire a local guide. Sources: Sorata tourism and La Paz tourism publication.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="-27.313500" lon="-55.987050">
    <name>Aguavista MTB Circuit (Paraguay) [no lift]</name>
    <desc>Organized cross-country MTB circuit at Aguavista in San Juan del Parana, Itapua. The circuit is about 5.1 km and hosts UCI-level XCO events. The pin marks the official 2026 course start/finish beside the Aguavista tennis courts at about 120 m. Sources: Paraguay National Sports Secretariat, the official 2026 technical guide and OpenStreetMap tennis-court ways 510311623, 510311624 and 510311625 (ODbL).</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="-34.080000" lon="-54.380000">
    <name>Cerro Aspero MTB (Uruguay) [no lift]</name>
    <desc>Marked mountain-bike circuit in the Cerro Aspero area of Rocha, with a roughly 40 km adventure route and a hike-a-bike approach to the summit. Natural, rolling terrain with no lift. Source: Rocha Tourism.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="29.166000" lon="-82.132000">
    <name>Santos Trailhead / Cross Florida Greenway [no lift]</name>
    <desc>IMBA Ride Center and extensive singletrack network around Santos, with more than 80 miles of mountain-bike trails, a pump track and skills park. Open year-round during park hours. Sources: Florida State Parks biking guide and Santos Trailhead information.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="27.805000" lon="-82.181000">
    <name>Alafia River State Park MTB [no lift]</name>
    <desc>One of Florida's most varied mountain-bike areas, built through reclaimed phosphate-mining terrain with unusually steep elevation changes for the state. Park trails are maintained and conditions can change; check closures before riding. Source: Florida State Parks - Alafia River.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="26.118000" lon="-80.360000">
    <name>Markham Park MTB Trails (Florida) [no lift]</name>
    <desc>Managed mountain-bike trail network at Markham Park in Broward County, with purpose-built trails and riding facilities. Check the park page for current access, conditions and rules. Source: Markham Park mountain-bike trails.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="39.310380" lon="-108.706010">
    <name>Fruita MTB - 18 Road and Kokopelli [no lift]</name>
    <desc>Fruita / Grand Valley desert singletrack destination

    Trails
Two adjoining, world-known riding areas: the North Fruita Desert / 18 Road system for fast, playful desert singletrack, and the Kokopelli Trail system near Loma for exposed ledges, slickrock, technical loops and long views over the Colorado River. Classics include Joe's Ridge, Kessel Run, Mary's Loop, Horsethief Bench and Rustler's Loop.

    Getting up / price
No lift. Pedal-accessed trailheads and desert roads; check land-manager rules, parking requirements and closures before riding.

    Open season
Rideable year-round when trails are dry. Avoid muddy conditions because desert clay can be damaged easily; summer heat requires an early start and extra water.

    Sources: Go Fruita trail overview, BLM / Recreation.gov Kokopelli Trails and Colorado Parks and Wildlife - Fruita.</desc>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="35.198000" lon="-111.650000">
    <name>Flagstaff MTB Network (Arizona) [no lift]</name>
    <desc>High-elevation ponderosa-pine riding around Flagstaff

    Trails
A broad network of singletrack and multi-use trails around Flagstaff, including the Arizona Trail, Schultz Creek, Elden and the urban trail system. The high-altitude pine forest provides varied cross-country and natural all-mountain riding, with volcanic terrain and long seasonal views.

    Getting up / price
No lift. Ride from signed trailheads and use the current forest and city maps. Some routes are shared-use and some Arizona Trail sections have local restrictions, so follow posted rules.

    Open season
Lower trails can ride much of the year, while snow and freeze-thaw affect higher routes in winter and spring. Check current closures and trail conditions before riding.

    Sources: Flagstaff mountain biking guide, City of Flagstaff trail map and Flagstaff Biking Organization.</desc>
  </wpt>
